# 4. Test Point Analysis (TPA) – Movie Booking Feature

**Test Point Analysis (TPA)** is a technique used to estimate testing effort by looking at the size and complexity of the software from a testing point of view. Instead of simply guessing how many hours testing might take, we identify things such as inputs, outputs, user interactions, interfaces and complexity, and use them to estimate the overall testing size.

For a new **Movie Booking** feature in BookMyShow, I would consider activities such as movie search, theatre selection, show selection, seat selection, customer details, payment and booking confirmation. Each of these adds to the amount of testing work. A more complex feature with more screens and external integrations would generally require more test effort.

### Advantages

**1. More systematic estimation:**
TPA gives a structured way to estimate testing effort instead of making a random guess.

**2. Useful for comparing projects:**
It can help the team compare the expected testing effort of a small movie-search feature with a larger end-to-end booking feature.

### Limitation

**TPA can be difficult when requirements are still unclear.** If the size or complexity of the new Movie Booking feature keeps changing, the initial effort estimate may also become inaccurate.

**In simple words:** TPA helps the test team understand **how big and complex the testing work is likely to be before deciding how much time and manpower will be needed.**

